# Appendix F — Publication formats and protocols

This appendix is the lookup route for the data formats that connect Literate's
publication stages. The conceptual route begins with
[Why publication is a separate pipeline](separate_publication_pipeline.html),
continues through
[Building a publication document](building_publication_document.html), and ends
with the screen and print renderers described in
[Publication on screen](publication_on_screen.html) and
[Typesetting the book](typesetting_the_book.html).

Publication is explicit. Ordinary source generation may produce chapter HTML, but
it does not invoke Pandoc, a browser, TeX, or the PDF compiler. The `latex` and
`pdf` commands consume the same parsed project while using a separate publication
model and output transaction.

## Format inventory

Literate uses several related but distinct publication representations:

- **Generated HTML** is one page per `.literate` document, with semantic fragment
  and annotation links. It is part of ordinary generation.
- **Markdown preview** is editor-hosted screen presentation over the current
  project snapshot. It may use unsaved buffers and does not write generated files.
- **The publication document** is a renderer-neutral, validated block tree for the
  complete ordered project.
- **Pandoc JSON** is the controlled in-memory bridge used to ask Pandoc for a
  LaTeX body. It is not an author-facing source format.
- **LaTeX publication** is one renderer-owned document plus any planned image,
  diagram, font, and license companions.
- **PDF publication** compiles a private copy of that LaTeX plan and publishes only
  the accepted PDF destination.

Screen and print share logical fragment and annotation identities, but they do not
promise identical layout, heading behavior, or interaction. See
[Shared annotations and identities](shared_annotations_identities.html) and
[What Literate does not promise](what_literate_does_not_promise.html).

## Controlled Pandoc JSON

[The controlled Pandoc bridge](publication-pandoc.html) constructs Pandoc data
after the complete project, fragment graph, annotations, links, and supported block
vocabulary have passed validation. Pandoc does not parse the original Literate
Markdown and does not decide fragment identity or project order.

The document envelope has this stable internal shape:

```json
{
  "pandoc-api-version": [1, 23, 1],
  "meta": {},
  "blocks": []
}
```

The fixed API tuple is a wire-format compatibility choice. Metadata is deliberately
empty; page geometry, fonts, palette, and other renderer policy cross typed options
instead of arbitrary document metadata.

The bridge admits paragraphs, plain blocks, headings, horizontal rules, block
quotes, ordered and unordered lists, ordinary code blocks, inline emphasis,
strong emphasis, strikeout, code, links, line breaks, fragment contributions,
annotations, and supported standalone assets. Any unsupported token type that
actually reaches the bridge diagnoses the source rather than disappearing or
passing through as executable markup.

`RawBlock` and `RawInline` are reserved for trusted renderer callbacks. Normal
parsing leaves HTML and TeX-like author text inert and does not recognize pipe-table
syntax; none of those spellings becomes raw Pandoc output. Contribution and asset
callbacks run only after complete validation succeeds. For the gate and its
all-or-nothing behavior, see
[Building a publication document](building_publication_document.html).

The production converter receives JSON on standard input and is invoked without a
shell using these conversion arguments:

```text
pandoc --from=json --to=latex --top-level-division=chapter --no-highlight
```

Pandoc returns a LaTeX body, not a standalone document. Literate supplies the
preamble, syntax presentation, destinations, geometry, fonts, colors, annotation
rail, and resource references. The Pandoc process is bounded, and an empty body,
nonzero exit, timeout, stream error, or excessive output fails publication before
commit.

## Annotation author format

Annotations are authored as exact, case-sensitive fenced-block forms. A term has a
key and a JSON-quoted display title:

````markdown
```TERM transaction "Output transaction"
A transaction publishes related outputs together, or preserves the previous set.
```
````

A note has no header arguments:

````markdown
```NOTE
This explanation belongs beside this point in the prose.
```
````

A term use is an explicit Markdown link:

```markdown
See the [transaction contract](term:transaction).
```

The contracts are:

- a term key matches `[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_.:-]{0,99}` and is case-sensitive;
- the required display title is a JSON string of at most 200 Unicode code points;
- `NOTE` takes no arguments;
- term destinations are decoded once and must resolve to exactly one definition;
- duplicate terms, missing or ambiguous uses, invalid headers, and unsupported
  bodies block publication;
- links in headings are rejected because replaying a heading in contents or running
  matter would replay annotation markers;
- there is no authored `INDEX` fence: term and fragment indexes are derived from the
  validated model.

Annotation bodies accept paragraphs, lists, emphasis, strong emphasis, inline code,
links, and line breaks. They reject headings, quotations, tables, images,
strikethrough, block code, nested annotation or Mermaid fences, and raw HTML. TERM
bodies remain in document flow. NOTE bodies become numbered screen asides or
controlled print-rail entries. The model and resolution rules are explained in
[Shared annotations and identities](shared_annotations_identities.html); the exact
parser is in [Shared publication annotations](publication-annotations.html).

## Font settings and manifest format

Root publication settings select five font roles:

```text
font-body
font-heading
font-code
font-fragment
font-note
font-code-ligatures
```

The family values are trimmed ASCII names from one to one hundred characters,
beginning with a letter or digit and continuing with letters, digits, spaces, dots,
or hyphens. They are names, not paths or TeX expressions. `font-code-ligatures` is
exactly `on` or `off`. Settings are read only from root `index.literate` `SETTINGS`
fences; the last valid value wins, while every invalid occurrence remains a
diagnostic. If `font-note` is omitted, it inherits the final body family.

An optional font manifest turns selected families into portable companions. Its
version is a stable data-format boundary, so it is stated explicitly. Version 1 has
this shape:

```json
{
  "version": 1,
  "families": {
    "Example Serif": {
      "regular": "fonts/serif-regular.otf",
      "bold": "fonts/serif-bold.otf",
      "italic": "fonts/serif-italic.otf",
      "boldItalic": "fonts/serif-bolditalic.otf",
      "licenses": ["fonts/OFL.txt"]
    }
  }
}
```

Every family entry requires all four faces and a nonempty `licenses` array. Unknown
fields are errors. Paths are safe project-root-relative paths, not paths relative to
the manifest. Font containers are limited to `.ttf` and `.otf`; all declared files
must exist as regular non-symlink files. Every entry is validated and protected from
output collisions, although only selected families are copied.

Italic and bold-italic faces may explicitly request bounded synthetic slant:

```json
{
  "file": "fonts/example-regular.ttf",
  "slant": 0.2
}
```

Only those two roles accept this object. `slant` must be a finite JSON number from
`0.01` through `0.3`. There is no implicit fallback, synthetic weight, directory
scan, font installation, or license interpretation. License bytes travel with the
selected fonts, but the publisher remains responsible for redistribution rights.
See [Portable publication resources](portable_publication_resources.html),
[Publication font settings](publication-settings.html), and
[Explicit portable font companions](publication-fonts.html).

## Asset metadata and author forms

The renderer-neutral asset record is:

```text
kind: "image" | "mermaid"
source: string
caption: string
at: { path, line, column }
```

For an image, `source` is the authored path. Image paths are resolved relative to
the chapter containing the image, decoded once, and required to remain inside the
project. A supported image is a standalone Markdown image using PNG, JPEG, or PDF;
the extension and binary signature must agree. The title becomes the plain caption,
falling back to plain alternative text. Inline images, formatted alternatives,
remote URLs, query strings, fragments, raw SVG, symlinks, and unsupported file types
are rejected.

For a Mermaid asset, `source` is the complete diagram source and the caption is
empty. A plain Mermaid fence requests a diagram; a named Literate fragment whose
language is Mermaid remains program code. Diagram publication requires an explicitly
selected browser and produces validated PDF and SVG companions in a private worker.
Books without diagrams need no browser.

Companion names are derived from content or role identities rather than source
basenames. Equal image bytes are retained once, while every source alias remains a
protected input. TeX, images, diagram products, portable fonts, and license files
join one output plan and one transaction. Old companions are not pruned
automatically, so move or distribute the containing export directory as a unit.

## Publication helper request

Editor publication uses a one-request external helper described in
[Isolating publication from the editor](isolating_publication_editor.html). Standard
input contains one JSON value and end-of-file terminates the request. The envelope
has exactly two top-level members:

```json
{
  "args": ["latex", "/project/root", "--output", "book.tex", "--json"],
  "documents": [
    { "path": "index.literate", "text": "# Book\n" }
  ]
}
```

`args` is an array of strings. Its first item is `latex` or `pdf`; `--json` is
required; help flags and all other commands are rejected. `documents` is an array of
`{path, text}` source snapshots. Open file-backed editor buffers take precedence
when the adapter constructs that array. Binary assets, manifests, fonts, and licenses
remain policy-checked disk inputs rather than entering the JSON snapshot. Images and
diagrams have dedicated byte budgets; font and license companions currently do not.

The helper emits no readiness text. Standard output is exactly one CLI response, and
standard error is treated as bounded diagnostic transport. The request and response
are independently size-bounded. Cancellation remains latched through the runner and
output transaction.

## Publication helper response

Successful helper output uses CLI response schema version 1:

```json
{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "command": "latex",
  "ok": true,
  "root": "/project/root",
  "diagnostics": [],
  "errors": [],
  "outputs": [
    { "path": "book.tex", "status": "written" }
  ],
  "publication": {
    "warnings": []
  }
}
```

`command` is the requested `latex` or `pdf` command. Output status is one of
`current`, `missing`, `stale`, or `written`; a successful editor publication requires
the selected destination to be `written` or `current`. PDF results may additionally
report `publication.buildDirectory` when private build retention was requested.
`publication.warnings` contains deduplicated compiler warnings and does not turn a
successful build into a clean-typography guarantee.

A transport success is not enough. The editor also requires process close, a zero
exit status, valid JSON, schema version 1, the matching command, `ok: true`, and the
selected output status. A failure before commit preserves the previous output. A
cancellation arriving after a validated commit does not turn success into failure.
The shared machine-readable contract for all commands is documented in
[Command-line generation](cli.html).

## LaTeX integration contract

Literate owns publication template version 1. This version identifies the emitted
renderer template; it is not a custom-template extension point. The renderer expects
Pandoc to provide only a body, uses top-level chapters, and disables Pandoc syntax
highlighting so Literate can preserve fragment-aware syntax and links.

The template targets LuaLaTeX and uses a one-sided KOMA-Script book with controlled
font, geometry, color, code, image, link, index, and annotation-rail support. Text is
escaped as data. Logical destinations are encoded again before entering LaTeX; raw
fragment names, paths, and annotation keys are never interpolated as commands.

Page choices are `a4`, `a5`, `letter`, and `legal`. Margins are decimal millimetres,
each from 5 through 50, and must leave at least an 80 × 100 mm body. An annotated
book reserves a right-hand rail and gap in addition to the author's requested margin,
so geometry that works for an unannotated book may be rejected for an annotated one.

`latex` writes the TeX document and planned companions but never starts TeX. `pdf`
stages that same plan privately, preflights the compiler tools and packages, checks
reference and annotation-rail convergence, validates bounded PDF bytes, and then
publishes only the requested PDF. The exact safety and cancellation boundary is in
[Compiling and publishing a PDF](compiling_publishing_pdf.html).

## Failure lookup

- **Publication rejects the document before Pandoc:** check for inline images or token
  extensions outside the controlled vocabulary. Raw HTML and TeX are normally inert
  text, and pipe-table syntax is not recognized by the parser.
- **A term link is inert or publication fails:** check exact key case, duplicate or
  missing definitions, percent encoding, and whether the use appears in a heading.
- **A font manifest fails before rendering:** check `version: 1`, exact family names,
  all four faces, a nonempty license list, safe root-relative paths, and unknown
  fields.
- **An image is rejected:** make it a standalone PNG, JPEG, or PDF with a matching
  signature and a chapter-relative local path.
- **Mermaid export asks for a browser:** either supply the explicit browser option or
  remove ordinary Mermaid fences; named Mermaid fragments do not require rendering.
- **The editor rejects helper success:** require a matching schema, command, output
  path, and `written` or `current` status, not merely exit code zero.
- **A PDF succeeds with warnings:** inspect the publication warnings and the actual
  pages; compilation success does not establish glyph coverage or good typography.
